### What is a window assigner?

The window assigner decides which window or windows each incoming element belongs to. You set it with the `.window(...)` call. Different assigners produce different window shapes.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.*;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;

// Tumbling: each element in exactly one 1-minute window
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)));

// Sliding: each element in multiple overlapping windows
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(SlidingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1), Time.seconds(10)));

// Session: elements grouped by gap
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(5)));

// Global: all elements in one window, needs custom trigger
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(GlobalWindows.create());
```

### What is a trigger?

The trigger decides when the window function actually runs. It returns a `TriggerResult`: CONTINUE (do nothing), FIRE (emit but keep window), PURGE (clear window), or FIRE_AND_PURGE (emit then clear).

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.triggers.*;

// EventTimeTrigger: fires when watermark passes window end (default)
stream.window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .trigger(EventTimeTrigger.create())
    .sum("amount");

// CountTrigger: fires every N elements
stream.window(GlobalWindows.create())
    .trigger(CountTrigger.of(100))
    .sum("amount");

// ProcessingTimeTrigger: fires on wall clock
stream.window(TumblingProcessingTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .trigger(ProcessingTimeTrigger.create())
    .sum("amount");
```

```text
TriggerResult values:
  CONTINUE       → do nothing, keep accumulating
  FIRE           → run window function, keep window contents
  PURGE          → clear window contents, do not emit
  FIRE_AND_PURGE → run window function, then clear contents
```

### How do you build a custom trigger?

You can override the default trigger to fire early for speculative results and then finally on the watermark.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.triggers.Trigger;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.triggers.TriggerResult;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.windows.TimeWindow;

public class EarlyFiringTrigger
        extends Trigger<Transaction, TimeWindow> {

    private final int earlyCount;

    public EarlyFiringTrigger(int earlyCount) {
        this.earlyCount = earlyCount;
    }

    @Override
    public TriggerResult onElement(Transaction txn, long timestamp,
            TimeWindow window, TriggerContext ctx) {
        // Register event-time timer for final firing
        ctx.registerEventTimeTimer(window.maxTimestamp());
        // Count elements, fire early every earlyCount
        long count = ctx.getPartitionedState(
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("count", Long.class, 0L)).value();
        count++;
        if (count >= earlyCount) {
            ctx.getPartitionedState(
                new ValueStateDescriptor<>("count", Long.class, 0L))
                .update(0L);
            return TriggerResult.FIRE;  // speculative result
        }
        return TriggerResult.CONTINUE;
    }

    @Override
    public TriggerResult onEventTime(long time, TimeWindow window,
            TriggerContext ctx) {
        return TriggerResult.FIRE_AND_PURGE;  // final result
    }

    @Override
    public TriggerResult onProcessingTime(long time, TimeWindow window,
            TriggerContext ctx) {
        return TriggerResult.CONTINUE;
    }

    @Override
    public void clear(TimeWindow window, TriggerContext ctx) {}
}
```

### What is an evictor?

The evictor is optional and removes elements from the window buffer before or after the window function runs. Built-in evictors include `CountEvictor`, `TimeEvictor`, and `DeltaEvictor`.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.evictors.*;

// CountEvictor: keep only the last 10 elements
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .evictor(CountEvictor.of(10))
    .sum("amount");

// TimeEvictor: keep only elements within the last 30 seconds
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .evictor(TimeEvictor.of(Time.seconds(30)))
    .sum("amount");
```

### How do the three components interact?

The interaction has a strict order: for each element, the assigner selects its windows and adds it to each window's buffer, the trigger is consulted and may say fire, and if an evictor is present it prunes the buffer before and after the window function runs.

```java
// Full composition: assigner + custom trigger + evictor
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1))) // assigner
    .trigger(new EarlyFiringTrigger(50))  // fire every 50 elements + final
    .evictor(CountEvictor.of(10))          // keep only last 10
    .apply(windowFunction);

// Execution order:
// 1. Assigner: event lands in [10:00, 10:01) window
// 2. Trigger: consulted -- CONTINUE or FIRE?
// 3. If FIRE and evictor present:
//    a. Evictor.evictBefore: prune buffer
//    b. WindowFunction.apply: compute over survivors
//    c. Evictor.evictAfter: prune again (rarely used)
```

### Why does using an evictor disable incremental aggregation?

Attaching an evictor forces Flink to buffer every raw element in the window rather than incrementally aggregating, because the evictor needs access to individual elements to decide which to remove. This disables the memory-efficient incremental path.

```java
// WITHOUT evictor: incremental aggregation
stream.window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .reduce((a, b) -> merge(a, b));  // only one accumulated value in memory

// WITH evictor: full buffer
stream.window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .evictor(CountEvictor.of(10))
    .reduce((a, b) -> merge(a, b));  // ALL elements buffered in memory!
// Memory usage: O(window_size) instead of O(1)
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using an evictor without understanding the memory cost is the most common mistake. Another is forgetting to set a trigger on a global window, which means it never fires.

```java
// WRONG -- global window without trigger, never fires
stream.window(GlobalWindows.create())
    .sum("amount");  // Never emits! No trigger defined.

// CORRECT -- global window with explicit trigger
stream.window(GlobalWindows.create())
    .trigger(CountTrigger.of(100))
    .sum("amount");  // Fires every 100 elements
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Only reach for an evictor when you genuinely need element-level pruning, because it costs memory. Otherwise prefer a plain reduce or aggregate which Flink can run incrementally. A custom trigger gives speculative early results and a final correct one, which is the standard pattern for real-time dashboards that want both speed and eventual correctness.
